  "narrative": {
    "top_frame": "One run is not a loss. It's a warning signal.",
    "what_this_means": "Jack Perkins held Cleveland to one earned run across six innings, striking out four without breaking. The Athletics managed six hits but couldn't convert five runners in scoring position across the game, leaving critical chances empty in the second and eighth. Luis Medina and Elvis Alvarado combined for two scoreless frames, shutting down any late rally math. The bullpen did its job. The offense didn't. A clean pitching line against a competitive opponent means nothing when the bats produce nothing in the moments that matter.",
    "what_to_watch": "This is where inconsistency becomes visible. The starter gave them a real game. One run through six frames is a platform, not a ceiling. But the lineup saw runners on in two critical windows and scored zero runs from either. That's not a slump. That's a choice made at the plate, inning after inning. The strikeouts piled up at thirteen while hits stayed flat at six. They had the pitching. They didn't have the execution. This team will keep losing games like this until someone breaks the cycle in the middle innings instead of chasing it late. Five left on base against one earned run allowed. That's the math that kills teams.",
